  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mongolia Energy Governance (MEG)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Mongolia):  USAID&apos;s Mongolia Energy Governance (MEG) activity will advance shared U.S. and Mongolian priorities to mitigate the impacts of climate change and expand renewable energy use. With the energy sector at the core of Mongolia&apos;s national security and economic prosperity, USAID efforts will foster a modern, competitive, and sustainable energy sector that is less dependent on Russia and China to meet domestic energy needs. To facilitate a low carbon transition, USAID will: improve the operational and financial performance of electric utilities; accelerate policy and regulatory market reforms, expand public engagement, and employ best-value procurement methods; and encourage the adoption of modern technologies by boosting private sector investments on renewable and cleaner fuels, smart grids, and energy-efficient systems.  USAID will advance the green energy revolution by engaging with the government, research institutions, private businesses, and the technology sector to encourage the adoption of renewable and low carbon energy sources and maximize the utilization of Mongolia&apos;s vast wind and solar energy base. To improve operations of electric utilities, USAID will enhance network upgrade and generation capability and the capacity to integrate new energy resources. USAID will assess cybersecurity vulnerabilities of electric utilities and develop a responsive investment strategy to reduce risks to the electricity grid and regional networks. To promote power market competitiveness, USAID will carry out public information and awareness campaigns and facilitate market-based retail electricity pricing. To ensure a gender-responsive transition to low carbon development, USAID will develop a strategic gender action plan, promote equal participation of women and men in utility operations, and support local pollution reduction policies.. .</narrative>
  </description>
